ARM: pmsa-v8: do not give user mode access to every peripheral

pmsav8_setup_io() maps everything that is not system RAM with
PMSAv8_AP_PL1RW_PL0RW, so a user process can read and write any peripheral in
the address space. On an SoC whose debug and access-control blocks live there,
a single stray store can disable the debug port until the next power-on -- the
board keeps running and simply stops answering SWD, which is a long way from
looking like a userspace bug.

A stack overflow in an editor is enough to do it.

Nothing legitimate depends on the old behaviour: userspace reaches peripherals
through the kernel, which runs at PL1, and /dev/mem is served by the kernel.

Signed-off-by: Mikhail Matveev <xtreme@rh1.tech>

--- a/arch/arm/mm/pmsa-v8.c
+++ b/arch/arm/mm/pmsa-v8.c
@@ -215,7 +215,20 @@
 	bar = start;
 	lar = (end - 1) & ~(PMSAv8_MINALIGN - 1);
 
-	bar |= PMSAv8_AP_PL1RW_PL0RW | PMSAv8_RGN_SHARED | PMSAv8_BAR_XN;
+	/*
+	 * Privileged only.
+	 *
+	 * This used to be PL1RW_PL0RW, which hands user mode read-write access to
+	 * every peripheral on the chip -- on this SoC that includes the debug and
+	 * access-control blocks, so one wild store from a user process can disable
+	 * the debug port until the next power-on. A stack-overflowing editor is
+	 * enough; the symptom is a board that stops answering SWD while it carries
+	 * on running perfectly.
+	 *
+	 * Nothing legitimate needs it. Userspace reaches peripherals through the
+	 * kernel, which runs at PL1, and /dev/mem is served by the kernel too.
+	 */
+	bar |= PMSAv8_AP_PL1RW_PL0NA | PMSAv8_RGN_SHARED | PMSAv8_BAR_XN;
 	lar |= PMSAv8_LAR_IDX(PMSAv8_RGN_DEVICE_nGnRnE) | PMSAv8_LAR_EN;
 
 	return __pmsav8_setup_region(number, bar, lar);
